(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-13"] = {"chem-12-13-m01":{"idea":"Amines are classed by how many alkyl or aryl groups are on nitrogen.","steps":["Ammonia is NH₃.","In (CH₃)₂NH, two hydrogens are replaced by two methyl groups.","Two groups on N make a secondary amine."],"others":"A primary amine has one group on N. The hydrogens count the other way: the secondary amine has one hydrogen left, not two. A tertiary amine has three groups on N.","trap":"Count the groups on N, not the hydrogens.","answer":"a secondary amine, as two alkyl groups are on the nitrogen"},"chem-12-13-m02":{"idea":"The IUPAC name of an amine changes -e of the parent to -amine.","steps":["The parent hydrocarbon is benzene.","Drop the final -e and add -amine.","The name is benzenamine.","Aniline is the accepted common name."],"others":"Aminophenol has an –OH group as well. Benzamide is C₆H₅CONH₂, an amide. Phenylamine oxide is not a proper name for aniline.","answer":"benzenamine"},"chem-12-13-m03":{"idea":"A second alkyl group on N gets the locant N.","steps":["The longest alkyl group gives the parent: methanamine.","The other methyl group is on nitrogen.","This is shown by N- before the name.","The name is N-methylmethanamine."],"others":"Dimethylamine oxide is not a valid IUPAC name. 2-aminoethane is a wrong form for a different structure. N,N-dimethylmethanamine is the tertiary amine, which has three methyls.","answer":"N-methylmethanamine"},"chem-12-13-m04":{"idea":"Two extra methyl groups on nitrogen are shown as N,N-dimethyl.","steps":["(CH₃)₃N has three methyl groups on one nitrogen.","One methyl gives the parent, methanamine.","The other two are on N, so N,N-dimethyl.","The name is N,N-dimethylmethanamine."],"others":"2-methylpropan-2-amine is the tertiary butylamine, a different compound with C₄. N-methylmethanamine has only two methyl groups. Trimethylamine oxide has an extra oxygen.","answer":"N,N-dimethylmethanamine"},"chem-12-13-m05":{"idea":"A nitro group is reduced to an amino group.","steps":["Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl reduces –NO₂ to –NH₂.","Catalytic H₂ with Ni also works.","So nitrobenzene gives aniline."],"others":"NaNO₂ with HCl makes a diazonium salt from aniline, which is the opposite step. Sn with NaOH is not the standard reducing mix. Nitric acid and O₂ are oxidising, not reducing.","answer":"tin and hydrochloric acid, or by H₂ with a nickel catalyst"},"chem-12-13-m06":{"idea":"Gabriel synthesis gives pure primary aliphatic amines.","steps":["Potassium phthalimide reacts with an alkyl halide.","The N can be alkylated only once.","Hydrolysis then releases RNH₂.","Aryl halides do not react by this substitution, so aromatic amines are not made."],"others":"Secondary amines are not the target. It does not give a mixture, since N is alkylated once only. Aromatic amines fail because aryl halides do not undergo this substitution.","trap":"Only primary aliphatic amines.","answer":"pure primary aliphatic amines, but not aromatic amines"},"chem-12-13-m07":{"idea":"Hofmann bromamide degradation removes the carbonyl carbon.","steps":["RCONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → RNH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O.","The C=O carbon is lost as carbonate.","The amine has one carbon fewer.","It is a primary amine."],"others":"RCH₂NH₂ would have the same carbons as the amide, which does not happen. The acid is not the product. Secondary amine is not formed; the product is primary.","trap":"Step-down reaction: one carbon lost.","answer":"a primary amine, RNH₂, with one carbon less than the amide"},"chem-12-13-m08":{"idea":"KCN adds one carbon, and reduction turns CN into CH₂NH₂.","steps":["C₂H₅Br + KCN → C₂H₅CN, which has three carbons.","Reduction: C₂H₅CN + 4[H] → C₂H₅CH₂NH₂.","The amine has three carbons.","It is propan-1-amine."],"others":"Ethanamine has the same carbons as the bromide, ignoring the carbon from CN. Butan-1-amine would add two carbons. Methanamine would be a loss of carbon, which is the Hofmann route.","trap":"Nitrile route: step up by one carbon.","answer":"one more carbon than ethyl bromide, so it is propan-1-amine"},"chem-12-13-m09":{"idea":"Ammonia and alkyl halides give amines that keep reacting.","steps":["NH₃ + RX gives RNH₂.","RNH₂ is also a nucleophile and reacts with more RX.","This gives R₂NH, R₃N and R₄N⁺X⁻.","So a mixture forms."],"others":"Only the primary amine would need no further reaction, which is not true. Only the tertiary amine or only the quaternary salt is not what happens. All of them form together.","answer":"a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines and a quaternary salt"},"chem-12-13-m10":{"idea":"Hydrogen bonding with water makes small amines soluble.","steps":["Nitrogen has a lone pair, and N–H bonds are present.","Amine and water molecules can hydrogen-bond.","The larger the alkyl part, the less soluble the amine.","Lower amines dissolve well."],"others":"Amines do not react with water to give alcohols. They are polar, not non-polar. The bonds with water are hydrogen bonds, not ionic bonds.","answer":"form hydrogen bonds with water molecules"},"chem-12-13-m11":{"idea":"More N–H bonds mean more hydrogen bonding and a higher boiling point.","steps":["The primary amine has two N–H bonds.","The secondary amine has one.","The tertiary amine has none.","So the order is primary > secondary > tertiary."],"others":"Boiling points are not the same, even though the molar mass is. Putting the secondary or tertiary amine first reverses the N–H count.","trap":"Same mass, more N–H means higher bp.","answer":"primary > secondary > tertiary"},"chem-12-13-m12":{"idea":"Amines boil lower than alcohols because their hydrogen bonds are weaker.","steps":["Both amines and alcohols form hydrogen bonds.","Nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen.","So N–H···N bonds are weaker than O–H···O bonds.","Less energy is needed to separate amines."],"others":"Nitrogen is less electronegative, not more. Amines are not ionic. They can hydrogen-bond; the claim that they cannot is wrong.","answer":"nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen, so the N–H···N bonds are weaker"},"chem-12-13-m13":{"idea":"A base here means a proton acceptor.","steps":["Nitrogen has a lone pair of electrons.","The lone pair takes H⁺ from water.","RNH₂ + H₂O ⇌ RNH₃⁺ + OH⁻.","The OH⁻ left over makes the solution basic."],"others":"The N–H bond does not give H⁺ in water in any important way. Nitrogen has no empty orbital to accept electrons. Alkyl groups do not release OH⁻.","answer":"the nitrogen has a lone pair of electrons that can accept a proton"},"chem-12-13-m14":{"idea":"In water, basicity depends on electron release and on hydration of the ammonium ion.","steps":["Alkyl groups push electrons to N and raise basicity.","The ammonium ion is stabilised by hydrogen bonds with water.","Hydration needs N–H bonds.","For ethyl groups, the secondary amine balances these best."],"others":"One group alone gives less electron release. Three groups give the most release, but little hydration, so the tertiary amine is weaker. A tertiary amine has no N–H bond, so its ion is poorly hydrated.","trap":"In water: secondary > tertiary > primary for ethyl amines.","answer":"has two electron-releasing groups, and the ammonium ion is well stabilised by hydration"},"chem-12-13-m15":{"idea":"The lone pair on N is less available in aniline.","steps":["The lone pair overlaps with the ring (resonance).","It spreads over the ring and is less free.","It is harder for it to accept H⁺.","So aniline is a weaker base than ammonia, pKb 9.38 against 4.75."],"others":"The ring withdraws electron density; it does not donate it. Aniline does have a lone pair. The lone pair is shared with the ring, not pushed by it.","answer":"the lone pair on the nitrogen is delocalised into the benzene ring"},"chem-12-13-m16":{"idea":"A smaller pKb means a stronger base.","steps":["pKb = −log Kb.","A smaller pKb means a larger Kb.","A larger Kb means more ionisation in water.","So the base with pKb = 3.4 is stronger."],"others":"A larger pKb means a smaller Kb, not a larger one. The two bases are not equal; a difference of 6 units is a million-fold difference in Kb. More OH⁻ comes from the smaller pKb.","trap":"Small pKb, strong base.","answer":"the one with pK_b = 3.4, as a smaller pK_b means a larger K_b"},"chem-12-13-m17":{"idea":"The carbylamine test needs a primary amine.","steps":["R–NH₂ + CHCl₃ + 3KOH → R–NC + 3KCl + 3H₂O.","R–NC is an isocyanide with a foul smell.","Two hydrogens on N are needed.","Secondary and tertiary amines do not react."],"others":"Secondary amines do not give the test. The product is not a pleasant-smelling ester. Tertiary amines have no N–H and give nothing.","trap":"Foul smell = primary amine.","answer":"primary amines only, and it gives a foul-smelling isocyanide"},"chem-12-13-m18":{"idea":"A primary amine gives a sulfonamide with an acidic N–H.","steps":["RNH₂ + C₆H₅SO₂Cl → C₆H₅SO₂NHR + HCl.","The N–H is acidic because of the sulfonyl group.","Alkali removes this H and forms a soluble salt.","So the product dissolves in alkali."],"others":"Primary amines are nucleophiles and do react. The sulfonamide of a primary amine does have an N–H. A sulfonamide with no N–H comes from secondary amines and does not dissolve in alkali.","answer":"a sulfonamide that dissolves in alkali, as it has an acidic N–H hydrogen"},"chem-12-13-m19":{"idea":"The reaction needs an N–H to lose HCl.","steps":["A tertiary amine has no hydrogen on N.","So it cannot lose HCl to form a sulfonamide.","It stays unchanged.","It does not dissolve in alkali and forms a separate layer."],"others":"A quaternary salt is not the product. Sulfonamides need at least one N–H, so the tertiary amine gives none. A tertiary amine has no N–H, so a sulfonamide with one N–H is wrong.","answer":"does not react with benzenesulfonyl chloride, as it has no N–H hydrogen"},"chem-12-13-m20":{"idea":"Primary aromatic amines form diazonium salts in the cold.","steps":["NaNO₂ + HCl gives HNO₂.","C₆H₅NH₂ + NaNO₂ + 2HCl → C₆H₅N₂⁺Cl⁻ + NaCl + 2H₂O.","The temperature is kept at 273–278 K.","The product is benzenediazonium chloride."],"others":"Nitrobenzene would need an oxidant. Phenol forms only when the diazonium salt is warmed. Benzene would come from a reducing agent such as H₃PO₂.","trap":"Keep it cold: the salt decomposes when warm.","answer":"benzenediazonium chloride"},"chem-12-13-m21":{"idea":"The Sandmeyer reaction replaces N₂⁺ by Cl, Br or CN.","steps":["Benzenediazonium chloride with CuCl and HCl is warmed.","N₂ gas leaves.","Cl takes its place on the ring.","The product is chlorobenzene."],"others":"An azo dye keeps N=N and is made by coupling, not here. Aniline would need reduction. Phenol comes from warming the diazonium salt with water, not from CuCl.","trap":"CuCl, CuBr, CuCN: Cl, Br, CN.","answer":"chlorobenzene, with the loss of N₂"},"chem-12-13-m22":{"idea":"This is the coupling reaction.","steps":["The diazonium ion is a weak electrophile.","Phenol in base becomes phenoxide, which is very reactive.","The diazonium ion attacks the para position.","The product keeps –N=N– and is a coloured azo dye."],"others":"The colourless ester option ignores the dye. An amide is wrong. Chlorobenzene comes from the Sandmeyer reaction and loses the diazo group, while coupling keeps it.","answer":"a coloured azo dye, which keeps the –N=N– group"},"chem-12-13-m23":{"idea":"–NH₂ is a very strong activating group.","steps":["–NH₂ activates the ortho and para positions.","With bromine water, all three positions react.","No catalyst is needed.","2,4,6-tribromoaniline is a white precipitate."],"others":"3,5-dibromoaniline has the wrong positions. 4-bromoaniline is a single substitution, which needs controlled conditions. The ring is activated, not deactivated.","trap":"White precipitate with bromine water: aniline or phenol.","answer":"a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromoaniline"},"chem-12-13-m24":{"idea":"Hofmann elimination follows the opposite rule to Zaitsev.","steps":["The leaving group is a bulky trialkylamine.","The base takes the most accessible hydrogen.","That hydrogen is on the least substituted carbon.","So the least substituted alkene is the major product."],"others":"Zaitsev's rule gives the most substituted alkene, but not here. The alkenes are not formed in equal amounts. No alcohol forms, as the reaction is elimination.","trap":"Hofmann: least substituted. Zaitsev: most substituted.","answer":"the least substituted alkene, which is the opposite of Zaitsev's rule"},"chem-12-13-m25":{"idea":"In strong acid the amino group is protonated.","steps":["Conc. HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ make the mixture strongly acidic.","Aniline becomes the anilinium ion, C₆H₅NH₃⁺.","–NH₃⁺ withdraws electrons and directs to meta.","So a large amount of m-nitroaniline forms."],"others":"The acid does not activate the ring. The –NH₂ group is not oxidised to –NO₂ in this process. –NH₂ itself is ortho, para directing, not meta in all conditions.","answer":"the NH₂ group is protonated to –NH₃⁺, which directs to the meta position"},"chem-12-13-n01":{"idea":"For a weak base, [OH⁻] = √(Kb × c).","steps":["[OH⁻] = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.10) = 1.34 × 10⁻³ M.","pOH = −log(1.34 × 10⁻³) = 2.87.","pH = 14 − 2.87 = 11.1.","Check: the exact quadratic also gives 11.1."],"answer":"11.1"},"chem-12-13-n02":{"idea":"Find the moles of amide, the theoretical yield, then the percent yield.","steps":["Moles of amide = 7.3 / 73 = 0.10 mol.","1 mol of amide gives 1 mol of amine.","Theoretical mass = 0.10 × 45 = 4.50 g.","Actual mass = 4.50 × 0.80 = 3.60 g."],"answer":"3.60 g"},"chem-12-13-n03":{"idea":"Group the isomers by the number of alkyl groups on N.","steps":["Primary: propan-1-amine and propan-2-amine (2).","Secondary: N-methylethanamine (1).","Tertiary: N,N-dimethylmethanamine (1).","Total = 2 + 1 + 1 = 4."],"answer":"4"},"chem-12-13-n04":{"idea":"Aniline and sodium nitrite react 1 to 1.","steps":["Moles of aniline = 9.3 / 93 = 0.10 mol.","Moles of NaNO₂ = 0.10 mol.","Mass = 0.10 × 69 = 6.9 g.","Check: 9.3 × 69 / 93 = 6.9 g."],"answer":"6.9 g"},"chem-12-13-n05":{"idea":"One mole of aniline gives one mole of tribromoaniline.","steps":["Moles of aniline = 1.86 / 93 = 0.020 mol.","Moles of product = 0.020 mol.","Mass = 0.020 × 330 = 6.60 g.","Check: 6 × 12 + 4 + 3 × 80 + 14 = 330."],"answer":"6.60 g"},"chem-12-13-n06":{"idea":"Get [OH⁻] from the pH, then use Kb = [OH⁻]² / (c − [OH⁻]).","steps":["pOH = 14 − 11.0 = 3, so [OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻³ M.","Base left = 0.10 − 0.001 = 0.099 M.","Kb = (10⁻³)² / 0.099 = 1.01 × 10⁻⁵.","Answer in units of 10⁻⁵: 1.01."],"answer":"1.01 × 10⁻⁵"},"chem-12-13-n07":{"idea":"A primary amine reacts 1 to 1 with HCl.","steps":["Moles of HCl = 0.50 × 0.0200 = 0.0100 mol.","Moles of amine = 0.0100 mol.","Molar mass = 0.59 / 0.0100 = 59 g/mol.","This fits C₃H₉N: 36 + 9 + 14 = 59."],"answer":"59 g/mol"},"chem-12-13-n08":{"idea":"Here the base ionises too much for the shortcut, so solve the quadratic.","steps":["Kb = x² / (c − x), so x² + Kb x − Kb c = 0.","x = [−Kb + √(Kb² + 4 Kb c)] / 2.","x = [−4.4 × 10⁻⁴ + √(1.936 × 10⁻⁷ + 1.76 × 10⁻⁵)] / 2 = 1.89 × 10⁻³ M.","pOH = 2.72, so pH = 14 − 2.72 = 11.28."],"answer":"11.28"}};
